const ctid = `SELECT ctid, product_id, current_price
FROM products
WHERE product_id = 'P7';
--   ctid  | product_id | current_price
-- --------+------------+---------------
--  (12,3) | P7         |         20.00`;
const update = `BEGIN;
UPDATE products
SET current_price = 22.00
WHERE product_id = 'P7';
COMMIT;`;
const asyncCommit = `BEGIN;
SET LOCAL synchronous_commit = off;
UPDATE page_views SET views = views + 1 WHERE page_id = 42;
COMMIT;`;
const docs = 'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/';
---
<Base title="Storage and durability" description="How PostgreSQL stores rows in 8 kB pages, why COMMIT waits for the write-ahead log, and what a crash, a torn page or a drive that answers too early can take away.">
  <article class="visual-essay relational-design storage-essay">
    <StorageDefs />
    <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Storage and durability</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational deep dive</span><span>About 20 minutes</span><span>PostgreSQL 18</span></div></header>

    <p><strong>Durability</strong> is the promise that a committed change survives a crash. Once PostgreSQL tells your application that <code>COMMIT</code> succeeded, the change must still be there after a power cut, a kernel panic or a killed server process. PostgreSQL keeps that promise through the way it stores data: how rows sit on disk, how they are copied into memory to be changed, and what it writes to disk before it replies.</p>
    <p>Most of the time your application can take that promise for granted, but it reaches your code in a few places. If a connection drops just after you send <code>COMMIT</code>, you can’t tell whether the change was kept, so a retry has to be safe either way. A report that runs for hours can make busy tables keep growing, because PostgreSQL can’t clear away old copies of rows the report might still need. And writes you could afford to lose, such as page-view counts, can trade a fraction of a second of safety for faster commits. Come back to this article when you’re deciding how your application retries, chasing a table that won’t stop growing, or choosing a server’s write settings and disks.</p>
    <p>It starts with how PostgreSQL stores a row, because that stored form is what has to reach the disk. Every example follows one change. A shop’s <code>products</code> table holds P7, a blue mug priced at 20.00, and an <code>UPDATE</code> raises it to 22.00. The examples use PostgreSQL 18’s default settings on one server.</p>
    <Sql code={update} label="The price change every example follows" />
    <p>Memory is fast and lost in a power cut. Disk survives, but every write to it is slow. So where does P7 live, and what has to reach the disk before PostgreSQL can say <code>COMMIT</code>?</p>

    <nav class="storage-contents" aria-label="In this article">
