    <section aria-labelledby="pages"><h2 id="pages">Pages and the heap</h2>
      <p>To see what has to reach the disk, start with how a table is laid out there. A <a href={`${docs}storage-page-layout.html`}><strong>page</strong></a> is the 8 kB unit PostgreSQL stores and reads. Every table is a file of pages, and every row lives inside one page. To read one row, PostgreSQL reads the whole page that holds it.</p>
      <p>Each page starts with a 24-byte header. Next comes an array of <strong>line pointers</strong>, 4 bytes each, one per row. Each pointer says where its row sits on the page, so a row can move within the page without changing its address. Rows fill the page from the other end. Press play to fill an empty table, then try a different row size.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="page-fill"
        number={1}
        title="Rows and line pointers filling three pages"
        alt="Three 8 kB pages drawn as tall columns. Line pointers grow down from each header; rows stack up from the bottom."
        caption="Drawn to scale, except the 24-byte header, drawn thicker so it stays visible. A 100-byte row takes 132 bytes: a 24-byte row header, the data, 4 bytes of padding so the next row starts on an 8-byte boundary, and its pointer. The rows have no NULL values."
      />
      <p>The wider the row, the fewer fit on a page. A row can’t span two pages, so PostgreSQL compresses values wider than about 2 kB or moves them to a separate <a href={`${docs}storage-toast.html`}><strong>TOAST</strong></a> table.</p>
      <p>An ordinary PostgreSQL table is a <strong>heap</strong>: each row goes wherever there is room, in no particular order. A row’s address is its <strong>TID</strong>, a page number and an item number, which is the number of its line pointer. Queries can read it as the hidden <code>ctid</code> column. P7 is at <code>(12,3)</code>, page 12, item 3:</p>
      <Sql code={ctid} label="Ask PostgreSQL where P7 is stored" />
      <p>An index on <code>product_id</code> stores that TID, which is how PostgreSQL finds P7 without reading the whole table. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">Indexes</a> covers that part.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="buffers"><h2 id="buffers">Shared buffers (the buffer cache)</h2>
      <p><a href={`${docs}runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-SHARED-BUFFERS`}><strong>Shared buffers</strong></a> are PostgreSQL’s cache of pages: a fixed pool of 8 kB slots in memory, shared by every connection. PostgreSQL works only on pages in shared buffers, so to read P7 it first needs page 12 in a slot. The default pool is 128 MB, or 16,384 pages. Watch a few queries ask for products.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="buffer-read"
        number={2}
        title="Four buffer slots above the table file"
        alt="Four buffer slots in memory above pages of the table file on disk."
        caption="Four slots stand in for 16,384. Real lookups also read index pages through the same buffers; this drawing counts only table pages."
      />
      <p>The first query for page 12 paid for a <strong>read</strong>; every later query for it was a <strong>hit</strong> and never left memory. Once every slot was taken, the next read had to <strong>evict</strong> a page to reuse its slot. <a href={`${docs}sql-explain.html`}><code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)</code></a> reports these counts on each plan node, in a line such as <code>Buffers: shared hit=3 read=1</code>.</p>
      <p>A read doesn’t always reach the drive. The operating system keeps its own cache of recently used file data, and PostgreSQL relies on it, which is why the documentation suggests starting shared buffers at 25% of RAM on a dedicated server.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="versions"><h2 id="versions">Row versions (MVCC)</h2>
      <p>An <code>UPDATE</code> doesn’t change P7 in place. It writes a new <a href={`${docs}mvcc-intro.html`}><strong>row version</strong></a>, a second stored copy of the row, and marks the old one as replaced. That new version is the change that has to be made durable. PostgreSQL’s own word for a version is <strong>tuple</strong>. Both versions stay in the table for a while, and each query decides which one it may see.</p>
      <p>Every version records two transaction IDs. <strong>xmin</strong> is the transaction that created it. <strong>xmax</strong> is the transaction that replaced or deleted it, if any. A query compares them with its <strong>snapshot</strong>, a note of which transactions had committed when it started. A report that needs consistent figures can keep one snapshot for its whole transaction; the <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> isolation level does this. Watch a report and a new query read P7 while transaction 812 changes the price.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="row-versions"
        number={3}
        title="Two readers and an update on page 12"
        alt="Page 12 as a table of items with their xmin and xmax, and letters marking what each reader reads."
        caption="A real row header also points from the old version to the new one."
      />
      <p>The report kept reading 20.00 after the update committed, the new query read 22.00, and neither waited for the other. This is <strong>multiversion concurrency control</strong>, or MVCC: readers don’t block writers, and writers don’t block readers. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/isolation/">Isolation and snapshots</a> explains which snapshot each isolation level takes. The cost is that old versions pile up. The next section shows how they’re cleared.</p>
      <p class="example-note">To watch <code>xmin</code> and <code>ctid</code> change on a real table, try the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-read-write">read and write lesson</a> in the browser sandbox.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="vacuum"><h2 id="vacuum">VACUUM and HOT updates</h2>
      <p><a href={`${docs}routine-vacuuming.html`}><strong>VACUUM</strong></a> removes <strong>dead</strong> row versions, ones no transaction can see any more, and makes their space reusable. <strong>Autovacuum</strong> runs VACUUM for you as dead versions build up. Watch P7’s price change five times on a page with room for six versions.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="vacuum"
        number={4}
        title="Pruning, with and without an old snapshot"
        alt="An index on product_id above pages 12 and 13, each with six slots for row versions."
        caption="Six versions per page keeps the drawing small; a real page holds dozens. Run it once, then again with a long report running."
      />
      <p>When page 12 filled, the next update cleared the dead versions on the spot. This is <strong>pruning</strong>, and any query or update that visits a nearly full page can do it. Each new version fit on page 12 and no indexed column changed, so the index needed no new entry. That makes all five <a href={`${docs}storage-hot.html`}><strong>HOT updates</strong></a>, short for heap-only tuple. Pruning keeps item 3’s line pointer as a redirect to the live version, so the index entry still finds P7.</p>
      <p>The open report blocked all of that. PostgreSQL can remove an old version only after every snapshot that could see it has closed, and the report might still need every one. So the 25.00 version went to page 13 and the index gained an entry. Once the report finished, VACUUM cleared the dead versions and the index entry for the original <code>(12,3)</code>.</p>
      <p>One report left running for hours holds back cleanup on every table in its database, and busy tables grow while it runs. VACUUM gives space back to the table, not to the operating system: the file keeps its size and new rows fill the gaps. <code>VACUUM FULL</code> rewrites the table to shrink it, but locks the table while it runs.</p>
      <p>HOT needs free space on the page. A table’s <a href={`${docs}sql-createtable.html#RELOPTION-FILLFACTOR`}><strong>fillfactor</strong></a> keeps some back on purpose: at 90, inserts fill each page only to 90%, and the rest is left for updates. The default is 100.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dirty"><h2 id="dirty">Dirty pages</h2>
      <p>A <strong>dirty page</strong> is a page in shared buffers that has changed since it was last written to disk. After the update, page 12 in memory holds the 22.00 version, while page 12 in the table file still holds only 20.00. A dirty page also has to be written out before its slot can be reused for another page.</p>
      <p>Suppose a database replied to <code>COMMIT</code> while the only copy of the new price was still in memory. PostgreSQL doesn’t work this way, and the figure shows why.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="dirty-crash"
        number={5}
        title="A database without a log"
        alt="Two lanes: memory, holding page 12 in shared buffers, above disk, holding page 12 in the table file."
        caption="A hypothetical design, shown for contrast. A coral outline and the word ‘dirty’ mark a page whose memory copy differs from the file."
      />
      <p>The application was told the change committed, and the change is gone. The obvious fix is to write page 12 to the table file before replying. That’s slow: one transaction can dirty pages across the table and its indexes, each a separate 8 kB write to a different place on disk. It isn’t safe either, because a page half-written when the power fails is part old and part new. <a href="#torn-pages">Torn pages</a> comes back to that.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="wal"><h2 id="wal">Write-ahead log (WAL)</h2>
      <p>The <a href={`${docs}wal-intro.html`}><strong>write-ahead log</strong></a>, or WAL, is a record of every change PostgreSQL makes to a page, precise enough to repeat it. Records go first into <strong>WAL buffers</strong> in memory, then into files in the <code>pg_wal</code> directory. Each record has a <strong>log sequence number</strong>, or LSN: its byte position in the log. Each page remembers the LSN of the last record that changed it. Step through the price change again, this time with the log.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="wal-commit"
        number={6}
        title="The price change with a write-ahead log"
        alt="The same lanes, with WAL buffers added to memory and the WAL file added to disk."
        caption="The drawing keeps the update record in memory until COMMIT; a background flush may write it to disk sooner."
      />
      <p><code>COMMIT</code> waited for one thing: the WAL, up to and including the commit record, had to reach disk. On Linux the flush is the <code>fdatasync</code> system call; <a href="#fsync">fsync and write caches</a> covers what it really promises. The table file still said 20.00 when the application heard <code>COMMIT</code>, and that’s fine, because the log on disk can rebuild 22.00.</p>
      <p>That order gives the log its name. Before a dirty page goes to the table file, the WAL up to that page’s LSN must already be on disk.</p>
      <p>Writing the log is cheaper than writing the pages, because every record goes on the end of the same file. One flush can also serve many transactions. When several commit at once, the first to flush writes out all the WAL inserted so far, and the others find their commit records already on disk. This is <strong>group commit</strong>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="recovery"><h2 id="recovery">Crash recovery</h2>
      <p><a href={`${docs}wal-internals.html`}><strong>Crash recovery</strong></a> replays the WAL after a crash to bring the table files up to date. It doesn’t start at the beginning of the log. It starts at the last checkpoint, a point where PostgreSQL knows the table files held every change logged before it.</p>
      <p>For each record, recovery compares the record’s LSN with the LSN on the page it changes. If the page is older, the change is missing and recovery applies it. If the page already has it, recovery skips it, so no change is applied twice. Choose when the power fails, then watch recovery.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="crash-recovery"
        number={7}
        title="Crash recovery at three moments"
        alt="The lanes from figure 6, with the power failing at the chosen moment."
        caption="Page 12 had already changed once since the last checkpoint, so the earlier records include it. Records still in memory at the crash are gone."
      />
      <p>Recovery brings back exactly the transactions whose commit records reached disk. A transaction that never committed may have its records replayed too, but its versions stay invisible, as they were before the crash.</p>
      <h3 id="dropped-connection">When the connection drops after COMMIT</h3>
      <p>If the connection drops after the application sends <code>COMMIT</code>, two different things could have happened. If the power failed before the flush, the change is gone. If it failed after the flush but before the reply, the change committed. Either way the application sees only a dropped connection. Retrying blindly could apply the change twice, which matters more for a payment than for a price. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Handling conflicts and uncertain outcomes</a> shows how to make a retry safe.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="checkpoints"><h2 id="checkpoints">Checkpoints</h2>
      <p>A <a href={`${docs}wal-configuration.html`}><strong>checkpoint</strong></a> picks a <strong>redo point</strong> in the WAL, writes every page dirtied before it to disk, then records that recovery can start there. After a crash, recovery needs only the log from the latest redo point onward.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL starts a checkpoint every 5 minutes (<code>checkpoint_timeout</code>), or sooner if WAL approaches <code>max_wal_size</code> (1 GB). It spreads the page writes over 90% of the interval (<code>checkpoint_completion_target</code>) so they don’t arrive in one burst. Below, price changes arrive for products on pages 10 to 15. Play it, then try a checkpoint every 20 records.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="checkpoints"
        number={8}
        title="Checkpoint interval"
        alt="Six pages in shared buffers, marked clean or dirty, above a grid of WAL records labelled with their pages. C marks a checkpoint."
        caption="Checkpoints here are instant and counted in records; real ones are timed and spread out."
      />
      <p>Every 5 records: 5 records to replay after the crash, and 30 pages written. Every 20 records: 20 to replay, and 6 pages written. A page that changes again after a checkpoint has to be written again, so frequent checkpoints write more.</p>
      <p>Checkpoints aren’t the only writers. The <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-resource.html#RUNTIME-CONFIG-RESOURCE-BACKGROUND-WRITER`}><strong>background writer</strong></a> writes some dirty pages early so that queries find clean slots, and a query that finds none writes one out itself. Neither moves the redo point.</p>
      <p>If the server log warns that checkpoints are occurring too frequently, raise <code>max_wal_size</code>. Once a checkpoint completes, crash recovery no longer needs the WAL from before its redo point. PostgreSQL recycles those WAL files, unless a backup archive or a replica still needs them.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="torn-pages"><h2 id="torn-pages">Torn pages and full_page_writes</h2>
      <p>A <strong>torn page</strong> is a page that was only partly written when the power failed. PostgreSQL writes 8 kB at a time, but a drive only promises to write each 512-byte sector whole. A page is 16 sectors, and the power can fail between any two of them.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL’s defence is <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-FULL-PAGE-WRITES`}><code>full_page_writes</code></a>, on by default. The first time a page changes after a checkpoint, the WAL record for that change carries a <strong>full-page image</strong>, a copy of the whole page. Watch a page write fail partway, with the setting on and then off.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="torn-page"
        number={9}
        title="A torn page and its full-page image"
        alt="Page 12 as sixteen 512-byte sectors, in shared buffers and in the table file, with its WAL record below."
        caption="New sectors are striped; old ones are plain. Drives don’t promise to write sectors in order; the drawing shows them in order for clarity."
      />
      <p>With the image in the log, recovery doesn’t need the torn page at all. It copies the image over it and replays later changes on top. One image per checkpoint is enough: recovery always starts at the last redo point, so the first record it meets for a page is the one carrying the image.</p>
      <p>Without the image, the page can’t be repaired. With data checksums, on by default in new PostgreSQL 18 clusters, recovery at least stops and reports it; without them the tear goes unnoticed. Turn <code>full_page_writes</code> off only if your storage guarantees whole-page writes, as ZFS does.</p>
      <p>Full-page images are the second cost of frequent checkpoints. Each one adds up to 8 kB of WAL, even when the change itself is a few bytes. Over figure 8’s 40 changes, a checkpoint every 5 records logs 35 images; every 20 records, 12.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="fsync"><h2 id="fsync">fsync and write caches</h2>
      <p><strong>fsync</strong> is the system call that asks the operating system to put a file’s data on durable storage and report when it has. <code>fdatasync</code> does the same while skipping metadata it doesn’t need. Every guarantee so far rests on that report. If the report is wrong, a committed transaction can vanish.</p>
      <p>Two caches sit between PostgreSQL and the storage itself. The operating system’s page cache holds data PostgreSQL has handed over but not yet flushed; it is the same cache that serves reads in <a href="#buffers">Shared buffers</a>. The drive has its own write cache, and on many drives that cache is ordinary volatile memory. Follow the commit record down, first with a drive that honours flush requests, then with one that doesn’t.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="flush-path"
        number={10}
        title="The flush path"
        alt="Four layers, top to bottom: WAL buffers, the operating system page cache, the drive’s write cache and its flash cells."
        caption="The top three layers are memory. An operating-system crash loses the top two; a PostgreSQL crash loses only the first."
      />
      <p>PostgreSQL couldn’t notice anything wrong with the second drive: it just answered too early. The <a href={`${docs}wal-reliability.html`}>PostgreSQL documentation</a> warns that consumer SATA drives and many SSDs have volatile write caches, and that many SSDs ignore flush requests by default. It advises avoiding disk controllers whose write cache has no battery, and turning off a drive’s write cache unless the drive guarantees the cache is written out before power is lost. Drives sold with <strong>power-loss protection</strong> make that guarantee: they hold enough stored energy to finish writing their cache.</p>
      <p>To check your hardware, run <a href={`${docs}pgtestfsync.html`}><code>pg_test_fsync</code></a>, which times each flush method. A disk spinning at 7,200 rpm turns 120 times a second, so it can complete at most about 120 flushes a second. A result in the thousands from such a disk means a cache is answering.</p>
      <p>On a managed service such as Amazon RDS or Google Cloud SQL, the provider chooses the drives and controllers, so these hardware checks are theirs to make. The settings in the next section, which decide whether <code>COMMIT</code> waits for the flush, are still yours.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="settings"><h2 id="settings">Asynchronous commit, fsync = off and unlogged tables</h2>
      <p><a href={`${docs}wal-async-commit.html`}><strong>Asynchronous commit</strong></a> lets <code>COMMIT</code> return before the WAL flush. You turn it on with <code>synchronous_commit = off</code>. PostgreSQL then replies as soon as the commit record is in the WAL buffers, and a background process, the <strong>WAL writer</strong>, flushes them soon after. The WAL writer wakes every 200 ms (<code>wal_writer_delay</code>). When the server is busy it prefers to write whole WAL pages, so a commit can wait up to three of those cycles: about 600 ms in the worst case.</p>
      <p>You can set <code>synchronous_commit = off</code> for a single transaction, for writes you could afford to lose, such as a page-view counter:</p>
      <Sql code={asyncCommit} label="Turn off synchronous commit for one transaction" />
      <p>Here is the price change with it off. Choose whether the power fails before or after the WAL writer runs.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="async-commit"
        number={11}
        title="The price change with synchronous_commit off"
        alt="The lanes from figures 6 and 7, with synchronous_commit off."
        caption="The same model as figures 6 and 7, with one setting changed."
      />
      <p>With <code>synchronous_commit</code> off, a crash can lose up to the last 600 ms of commits, and no more. Recovery replays commits in order, so the database stays consistent. It just ends slightly behind what the application was told.</p>
      <p>With a synchronous standby server, <a href={`${docs}runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-SYNCHRONOUS-COMMIT`}><code>synchronous_commit</code></a> also decides how far the standby must get before <code>COMMIT</code> returns, from <code>remote_write</code> (received) to <code>remote_apply</code> (applied, so queries there see it). <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-failover/">Replication and failover</a> covers those settings.</p>
      <p>Two other options look similar and are not. <code>fsync = off</code> stops PostgreSQL from flushing anything, data files included, and after an operating-system crash or power cut the database can be corrupt beyond repair. Use it only for a database you can rebuild from scratch. <a href={`${docs}sql-createtable.html#SQL-CREATETABLE-UNLOGGED`}><strong>Unlogged tables</strong></a>, created with <code>CREATE UNLOGGED TABLE</code>, skip the WAL. They are faster to write, and PostgreSQL empties them after any crash, committed rows included.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="backups"><h2 id="backups">Backups and point-in-time recovery</h2>
      <p>Durability protects committed changes from a crash. It doesn’t protect them from losing the disk, or from a mistake. If the drive holding the table files and <code>pg_wal</code> dies, there is nothing left to replay. If someone commits <code>DELETE FROM products</code>, crash recovery will faithfully keep the delete.</p>
      <p>A replica on another server covers a lost disk, but it copies the delete too. Undoing a mistake needs a copy from before it. A <strong>base backup</strong> is a copy of the data directory, and the <strong>WAL archive</strong> holds every WAL file written since. Restore the backup, replay the archive, and stop at a moment you choose, such as just before the delete. This is <a href={`${docs}continuous-archiving.html`}><strong>point-in-time recovery</strong></a>. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backups and recovery</a> covers what to keep and how to test a restore.</p>
    </section>

